  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThis forecast features no tricks, but honestly, not too much in the \nform of treats either as Halloween in South Florida is forecast to \nbe close to normal for this time of year. Warm daytime temperatures \nwill drop into the upper 70s to low 80s during the evening hours, \nwith a 30-50% chance of coastal showers along the east coast of \nSouth Florida and a light but not spooky easterly breeze. But before \nI haunt you any further with my forecast, let's rewind back to the \nbeginning of the extended period. \n\nThe atmospheric reign of the cut-off low that has resided to our \neast over the northwestern Bahamas and western Atlantic waters over \nthe last several days will finally conclude. While zonal mid-level \nflow will prevail across much of the southeastern United States, a \ndeveloping mid-level ridge across the southern Gulf will slide \neastward during the second half of the week, resulting in increasing \ngeopotential heights, 500mb temperatures above the 90-95th \npercentile, and a reduction in 500mb wind speeds out of the \nnorthwest. At the surface, the weak area of surface low vorticity is \nforecast continue to meander westward across the region, \nincreasingly decoupled from the departing cut-off low. An associated \nfrontal boundary, increasingly diffuse and meager in nature, will \nalso retrograde across the region during the second half of the \nweek. While this boundary will gradually degrade, the envelope of \ndeeper atmospheric moisture will remain across the region with \nprecipitable water values in the 80th-90th percentile forecast to \nremain draped across the region. The pressure gradient that has kept \na persistent breeze across the region will finally abate with high \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 80s across most of South Florida, \nwith even some low 90s possible across inland southwestern Florida \nthrough the upcoming weekend. While winds will continue to lessen \nacross the region, there will be enough of an easterly wind \ncomponent to create a minor temperature gradient across the region \nwith the warmest overnight temperatures forecast along the east \ncoast and warmest daytime temperatures across inland southwestern \nFlorida as oceanic influences keep temperatures warmer overnight but \ncooler during the day. Warm 500mb temperatures and ridging aloft \nwill keep convection fairly benign in nature through the first half \nof the weekend with convection mainly focusing across inland \nsouthwestern Florida on the confluence of a stronger Atlantic sea-\nbreeze and weaker Gulf breeze. \n\nMid-level ridging across our region is forecast to erode during the \nsecond half of the weekend into early next week as a lobe of mid-\nlevel vorticity slides eastward across the southeastern United \nStates in tandem with a developing surface low and frontal boundary. \nAmple warm air and moisture advection is forecast to occur mainly to \nthe north of our region during this time frame, with the best \natmospheric dynamics and ascent over the northern half of the \nFlorida Peninsula and remainder of the southeastern United States. \nThat being said, a cooling of 500mb temperatures and a more \nsoutherly component to surface winds could focus a higher chance of \nconvection across inland South Florida on Sunday as moisture lifts \nnorthwards in the expansive warm sector of the system to our north. \nBeyond early next week, ensemble and deterministic guidance have a \ngreat variety of potential forecast solutions as the southern stream \nremains active across the southern United States. In the near term \nhowever, it looks like South Florida will have to wait longer for \nthe treat of a substantial cold front and pumpkin spice\nconditions.",
